\version "2.24.0"
% c213 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 136: after a long rest horns re-enter pp or ff, mezzo-forte is colourless; PDF 127: the added group enters p and makes its crescendo faster): 89 Hn 1-4 enter p < (was mf <) into the ff at 91; Hn 3-4 had rested 46 bars. Notes unchanged.
% CLOSING GROUP, bars 83-98, E flat major, Tempo I, f to ff. 83-86: theme F1's head in major in the high winds and violins;
% 87-90: the head in the low strings and bassoons under the violins' turn figure (motive x), cadence in E flat at 90 (root V-I is
% allowed in E flat; the rule against it applies to C); 91-94: codetta chords, the brass's off-beat rhythm; 95-98: the E flat chord
% gains its seventh (V7 of A flat), then the bass falls E flat - D flat - C to V of F minor, diminuendo to p: the development starts in F minor.

% c032 (harmony; A&S PDF 267): 88-89 Tbn 2 D3-E flat3 | F3 (was F3-E flat3 | D3: 5ths with the bass's B flat-A flat at 88 and with Vln I's A-B flat at 89-90).
% c053 (form; A&S Unit 18, PDF 290-292, Ex. 18-2/18-3, Mendelssohn Op. 53/3: a descending series of 6/3 chords is a contrapuntal bridge from the tonic to the cadence;
% PDF 291: the upper voices may move in parallel 4ths; PDF 293: alternate doublings, leading tone not doubled): 87-90 no longer state F1's head in the low strings and
% bassoons. That layout (Vla, Vc, Cb, bassoons, f) opens the development nine bars later (99), so the development entered with the closing group's own idea and only the
% key marked the new section. 87-90 are now I6 - vii6 - vi6 - V6 - ii4/3 - V7 - I over the bass G-F-E flat-D-C-B flat-E flat (Vc, Cb, Bn 2, Cbn), the 6/3 voices in
% Vla (double stops), Tbn 1-2 and Bn 1, under the unchanged violin scale figure (89b: F, not D, under the violins' A-B flat, which would make fifths into 90); the horn off-beats at 89-90 follow the new chords (they had I on V and V on I); the 7th A flat of V7 is only in Bn 1 and Tbn 1 (unison), not doubled at the octave.
% c052 (harmony; A&S Unit 25, PDF 439 rule of doubling, PDF 405: the 7th resolves down): 96 the bass stays on E flat (E flat 7 in root position, its 7th D flat only in the top voice) instead of doubling the soprano's E flat-D flat-C in octaves; 97 Cl 1 C5, so the 7th B flat of C7 is not doubled.
% c054 (orchestration; Adler ch. 8, PDF 255, 257, 266): 87-90 Tbn 1-2 rest (they doubled Bn 1 and the violas' 6/3 voices at the unison, f, in the A3-E flat4 register of Vln II's
% scale figure); Fl 1 doubles Vln I's figure at pitch (without its low octave notes), so the figure carries; the trombones' first entry is now the ff codetta at 91.
% c058 (expression; Sebesky ch. 1, PDF 13-14 focus; ch. 6, PDF 208: push into the next phrase): 87 the violas' 6/3 double stops mf under the violins' and flute's figure (f); 89 horns mf; 89-90 crescendo in every part into the ff codetta at 91.
% c059 (holistic; Adler part 1 PDF 137 Ex. V-14, a crescendo built by adding octaves; PDF 250-251, 8'+4' doubling): 89-90 Vln I and Fl 1 an octave higher (F6-A flat5, B flat5-F6),
% Vln II stays on c057's line (F5-A flat4), so the crescendo into 91 keeps the two octaves it had in v49 while Vln II still avoids the 6/3 voices (c057).
% c059: 90 the bass E flat (Vc, Cb, Bn 1-2, Cbn) is held through beat 3 under the violins' F6/F5 and released for the pickup on beat 4 (Adler part 1 PDF 240: the held bass gives the cadence its weight;
% PDF 245, Strauss: the sustained part stops so the anacrusis is heard); with the bass gone on beat 3 the F6 over Vln II's F5 read as octaves with the E flats of beat 2.
% c098 (expression; A&S Unit 21, PDF 355 Ex. 21-17, Mozart K. 378: after the crescendo the next phrase starts at a new, lower level, marked on its first note): 95 the strings' E flat 7 had no
% dynamic of its own after the ff codetta and the half rest, so Vln I read ff > while Ob 1 played the same E flat5-D flat5 p; strings and the entering winds (Ob 1, Cl 1-2, Bn 2) now all start mf > to p at 97.
% c132 (harmony; A&S Unit 23, PDF 394, Ex. 23-12): 96 Cl 2 E flat4 (was G4 held), so E flat-E natural into the C7 at 97 is one voice; it was split between the bass E flat and Cl 2's E4.
% c144 (Adler ch. 8, PDF 235: oboe and clarinet in unison, the oboe covers the clarinet and the two are hard to keep in tune; PDF 257: unison doubling robs both timbres; PDF 270, Ex. VIII-41, Copland: a diminuendo scored by taking a doubling instrument out): 97-98 Cl 1 rests after its B flat4 (it held C5 p in unison with Ob 1 and Vln I); Cl 2 keeps the E of C7.
% c143 (form; A&S Unit 18, PDF 291-292, Ex. 18-2/18-3a, Mendelssohn Op. 53/3: the I6 that begins a 6/3 series is an expansion of the tonic, and only the series' end is a cadence point): 86 the bass
% resolves the seventh A flat of 85 to G (I6, Vc, Cb, Bn 2) and keeps 83-85's quarter-note arpeggio (G-B flat-E flat-B flat) into 87's G, the violas keep their eighths on I6 (G4-B flat4-E flat5, off the bass's octaves) and Bn 1 holds B flat3 through 85 (its A flat3 would double the bass's seventh A flat-G in octaves), so the F1 head no longer ends
% in a second root-position cadence in E flat (82, 86, 90) followed by a two-beat general pause; I6 at 86-87 starts the 6/3 bridge, 83-90 is one eight-bar phrase, and the closing group's only
% perfect cadence with the tonic on top is 93-94.
% c167 (texture; Sebesky ch. 3, PDF 168: in a large mixed ensemble the strings are the weakest element and are voiced as simply as possible in the high register; PDF 169-170 Ex. 215 and PDF 173 Ex. 216: the string chord rises above the brass; PDF 145: no double stops in the lead line): 91-94 Vln I play the codetta's top line G6-A flat6 | ... | E flat6-D6 | E flat6 single notes with Fl 1 (were double stops B flat4/G5-A flat5 inside the trumpets' and horns' register), and Vln II take Vln I's former double stops (were E flat4/B flat4, the violas' dyad at the unison).
% c218 (expression; Adler ch. 2, PDF 42-43, Ex. II-28 Gluck and Ex. II-29 Tchaikovsky 6/iii: a heavy, vigorous ff is a series of down-bows, the bow lifted between them, taken near the frog; PDF 38: an anacrusis is taken up-bow): 90-94 every accented quarter in Vln I, Vln II, Vla, Vc and Cb is a down-bow, the eighth pickups (91, 92) up-bow. By plain alternation 91.1 (after 90's two accented quarters), 92.1, 93.3 and 94.1 fell on up-bows. 90's two accented quarters are two down-bows, as at movement I 32 and finale 204. Notes, dynamics, layout unchanged (c167/c168 codetta).
