% c085 (Adler ch. 2, PDF 30: double notes are divided only when marked div.; 'non div.' when every player takes the double-stop): 87-90 the violas' two-voice suspension line is 'div.'; 91-94 the hammered chords 'non div.', as the violins play them.
% c147 (A&S PDF 76, 83, 405: the seventh is not doubled and resolves down; Rimsky PDF 112: parts in octaves strengthen one another): 85 beat 4 violas F4-B flat4-F4 (was A flat4-F4): their A flat4 doubled the bass arpeggio's passing seventh A flat3/A flat2 in octaves (F-A flat in Vla, Vc and Cb together), against c143's removal of Bn 1's A flat at 85; the seventh is now in the bass alone and falls to G at 86 (I6, the 6/3 bridge's first chord).
% c203 (form; A&S Unit 32, PDF 585: a chromatic inflection that is a motive of the piece is not softened, it draws the ear to the key change; PDF 597-598, Ex. 32-16/17 and note: Haydn prepares
% the pitch that later links the remote area by stating it prominently earlier, and ties C minor (E flat) to C major (E natural); PDF 600-601: the large plan enlarges the piece's motive):
% 95-98 the movement's first E natural (97, the third of C7 = V of F at the development's entry) was in Cl 2 alone, p. The violas now hold E flat4 under the E flat chord and inflect it to E4
% at 97 (double stop over their held B flat3; Cl 2 stays with them at the unison), so the E flat/E natural thread is a viola line from bar 1 (E flat4) through 97 (E4) to 219-220 (D sharp4-E4)
% and 223 (E4), at one pitch. Vln I and Ob 1 pass through D5 on 95 beat 4, so the top line 95-97 is E flat-D-D flat-C, movement I's motto tail in its second form (I 117-121, E flat 2. D 4 | D flat ...).
